        <mxCell id="CWc_muIFBfJnSlnraKPb-1" value="The ANCController is the biggest component of the project.&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&lt;div&gt;Its responsibility is managing the states and hence the interaction between&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;agent and environment. The controller applies the state pattern, meaning that it&#39;s&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&#39;chopped&#39; into multiple state objects. Each state object overrides the behavior of&amp;nbsp;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;the controller and can also decide its transitions to other states.&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;For example, RecordingState creates an Environment and KinestheticAgent&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;to record actions which later can be replayed by the ReplayRecordState.&amp;nbsp;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;This approach encapsulates logic for each state. A drawback is that the state pattern&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;is quite tightly coupled to the original object (anccontroller) and hence accessing variables&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;can feel a bit messy since Python doesn&#39;t care about access encapsulation.&amp;nbsp;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;States can &#39;share&#39; variables by making sure the self.context (controller) have them defined.&amp;nbsp;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;Therefor don&#39;t have any constructor(init) parameters. This feels ugly, but I have yet to figure&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&amp;nbsp;out if that&#39;s expected for the state pattern (feedback is welcome).&amp;nbsp;&lt;/div&gt;" style="text;html=1;align=left;verticalAlign=middle;resizable=0;points=[];autosize=1;strokeColor=none;fillColor=none;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="-255" y="-720" width="510" height="210" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
      </root>
    </mxGraphModel>
  </diagram>
</mxfile>
